WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.490
Sodium chloride, ordinary salt,

2
00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:05.250
has one sodium for every chlorine.

3
00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:09.270
But calcium chloride has two
chlorines for every calcium.

4
00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:10.530
Why not one?

5
00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:11.650
Why not three?

6
00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.290
Nobody chose these
numbers to look neat.

7
00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.570
Every ionic compound
has its own fixed ratio,

8
00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:19.970
written as small
numbers in its formula.

9
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.570
So what decides them?

10
00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.690
In this mission, you'll see
that ions carry charges,

11
00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:27.830
and learn how to work
out the charge on an ion.

12
00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:31.010
You'll find out what has to
happen to the charges when ions

13
00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:32.890
join to make a compound.

14
00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:36.290
And you'll see why those small
numbers must never change when

15
00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:38.010
you balance an equation.

16
00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:42.170
Your mission: work out what
decides the numbers in a formula.
